WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.071
There's a kind of wild paper from 2002 called Language Trees and Zipping,

00:00:04.071 --> 00:00:09.035
which shows how you can find structure between languages just by using very general file

00:00:09.035 --> 00:00:09.760
compression.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:14.622
For example, let's say I give you a bunch of text documents in many different languages,

00:00:14.622 --> 00:00:17.460
and your goal is to automatically cluster them by language.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.860
And actually, you can aim even higher than that.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.184
Suppose you also want to recover which languages are most closely related to each

00:00:24.184 --> 00:00:28.268
other in a way that lets you rediscover the tree of shared lineage between them,

00:00:28.268 --> 00:00:32.100
and all you can do is write a function that processes the text in each one.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.260
There's no pre-baked knowledge of linguistics.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.578
Now keep in mind, this is almost a quarter century ago,

00:00:37.578 --> 00:00:40.140
so nothing like modern language models is available.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.385
What's crazy is how the authors showed that you can do this just by using gzip,

00:00:44.385 --> 00:00:48.413
basically the same operation that you might use on your computer to make files a bit

00:00:48.413 --> 00:00:48.913
smaller.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.340
Here's the basic idea.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.394
Given two documents, A and B, append a small snippet of B to the end of A,

00:00:55.394 --> 00:00:56.960
and compress the result.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.200
Now compare that file size to what you get just by compressing A on its own.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.960
One way to think about this difference is it's telling you how well a little

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.860
snippet of B gets compressed when the compressor is mostly optimized for A.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.904
You'd expect that result to be small when the linguistic patterns of B

00:01:14.904 --> 00:01:18.600
are similar to those in A, but larger when B is more different from A.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.399
The authors used this general idea to define a specific metric for how far

00:01:23.399 --> 00:01:27.720
away two documents are, based exclusively on how well they compress together.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:31.212
And then, evidently, this co-compression-based distance

00:01:31.212 --> 00:01:34.040
was enough to recover the tree of language lineage.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:35.380
And it doesn't stop there.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.553
The same trick can be used for other natural language tasks,

00:01:38.553 --> 00:01:40.980
like identifying who authored a given document.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.597
What I like about this paper is what a pure example it is of when the theory and

00:01:46.597 --> 00:01:50.482
the engineering behind compression can be surprisingly useful to tasks which

00:01:50.482 --> 00:01:54.620
would seem to fall in the domain of machine learning and artificial intelligence.

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:58.700
The specific concept underlying this example is something known as cross-entropy.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:02.632
And what's interesting is that cross-entropy is also a very core part

00:02:02.632 --> 00:02:05.995
of how modern language models are trained, which gives a whisper of

00:02:05.995 --> 00:02:09.160
an unexpected connection between that training and compression.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.143
Most of what I want to do with this video is provide

00:02:12.143 --> 00:02:13.900
a lesson that goes back to the basics.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.914
We're going to spend the first half on the fundamentals of what cross-entropy is,

00:02:17.914 --> 00:02:21.740
how it naturally arises in the study of compression, and how you can visualize it.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:24.398
Then after that, we're going to go through the fundamentals

00:02:24.398 --> 00:02:26.560
on pre-training and distilling language models.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.229
And what's interesting is how, in that second half,

00:02:29.229 --> 00:02:32.460
the way that cross-entropy arises initially looks completely different.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.220
It seems unrelated to compression.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:38.086
But whenever you see the same formula pop up in two separate contexts,

00:02:38.086 --> 00:02:41.560
it's math's way of kind of winking at you and hinting at a connection.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.052
So at the end, armed with an understanding of where cross-entropy really comes from,

00:02:46.052 --> 00:02:48.264
combined with an understanding of pre-training,

00:02:48.264 --> 00:02:51.583
I want to show you how you can reframe the way you think about training

00:02:51.583 --> 00:02:54.395
large language models to not be about next token prediction,

00:02:54.395 --> 00:02:56.100
but instead to be about compression.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.766
It's easiest to motivate cross-entropy in the

00:03:04.766 --> 00:03:07.740
context of encoding messages into sequences of bits.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.467
This is a topic we began discussing in part one,

00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:13.898
where the framing is that we treat messages as sequences of symbols,

00:03:13.898 --> 00:03:18.075
something like characters of text, and if you treat each new symbol like it's being

00:03:18.075 --> 00:03:22.202
sampled from some probability distribution, those probabilities tell you something

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:24.440
about the fundamental limits on compression.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:27.858
Now, the case of natural language is incredibly complicated,

00:03:27.858 --> 00:03:30.516
those distributions would be very difficult to describe,

00:03:30.516 --> 00:03:33.920
so we centered a lot of our discussion on a very simplified toy example.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.311
And I want to pick up from that thread here, since

00:03:36.311 --> 00:03:38.280
that example also helps motivate cross-entropy.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:41.009
The setup was that we're sending sequences of

00:03:41.009 --> 00:03:43.620
four possible instructions to some faraway robot.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:48.454
Move up, down, left, or right, and each symbol we send is sampled from a distribution

00:03:48.454 --> 00:03:53.459
that gives a 50% chance to sending the instruction up, a 1 in 4 chance of sending down,

00:03:53.459 --> 00:03:57.440
and a 1 in 8 chance for each of the remaining symbols left and right.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.100
It's as if we want the robot to do some specific biased random walk.

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:06.087
One of the conclusions we came to in part one was how the best possible

00:04:06.087 --> 00:04:09.572
encoding here turns the symbol up into just a single bit,

00:04:09.572 --> 00:04:14.680
turns that symbol down into two bits, and turns left and right each into three bits.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:18.941
The whole point of this toy example is that it's a concrete illustration

00:04:18.941 --> 00:04:22.373
of a much more general fact, which is that for an optimal code,

00:04:22.373 --> 00:04:25.913
the number of bits that you allocate to a given symbol looks like

00:04:25.913 --> 00:04:29.720
the negative log base 2 of the probability for that symbol showing up.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.820
This negative log expression takes a little getting used to.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:36.754
I mentioned how I kind of wish that history had unfolded in such a way that we

00:04:36.754 --> 00:04:39.015
call it the log base 1 half of the probability,

00:04:39.015 --> 00:04:42.547
since it's really just asking how many times do you chop things in half to

00:04:42.547 --> 00:04:43.960
get down to a certain amount.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:49.160
Claude Shannon defined this expression to be the information content of an event.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:52.143
Now of course usually the numbers aren't so clean,

00:04:52.143 --> 00:04:55.049
so this information value is typically not a whole number,

00:04:55.049 --> 00:04:58.596
meaning your optimal codes can't look so simple as associating a symbol

00:04:58.596 --> 00:05:00.320
with some specific string of bits.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:04.510
The more general way to think about it is that to encode a full message,

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:08.252
the information content of the full message looks like the sum of the

00:05:08.252 --> 00:05:11.032
information contents of all the individual symbols,

00:05:11.032 --> 00:05:15.203
and the number of bits in an optimal encoding of the message is approximately

00:05:15.203 --> 00:05:16.860
that full information content.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.320
So fractional information really does have a very real meaning here.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:25.078
Now for part 2, imagine your space agency has decided to change the plan,

00:05:25.078 --> 00:05:29.344
effectively rotating things to encourage an overall more rightward journey,

00:05:29.344 --> 00:05:33.217
where from now on the symbols up and down each have a 1 in 8 chance,

00:05:33.217 --> 00:05:37.820
the symbol left has a 1 in 4 chance, and right is sampled fully half of the time.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:41.915
Supposing all of your encoding and decoding systems are hard-coded

00:05:41.915 --> 00:05:45.914
to use the previous encoding scheme, optimized for the old distribution,

00:05:45.914 --> 00:05:49.420
this is obviously now inefficient, but how inefficient exactly?

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:51.960
And this is not that hard to calculate.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:57.124
Now the single bit instruction for up comes up only one eighth of the time,

00:05:57.124 --> 00:06:01.018
the two bits for down also come up only one eighth of the time,

00:06:01.018 --> 00:06:04.426
and the other two instructions each require three bits,

00:06:04.426 --> 00:06:06.860
which together happens 75% of the time.

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:13.180
Adding this up as a weighted sum, you get an average of 2.625 bits per message.

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:16.881
You would say that this number represents the cross entropy

00:06:16.881 --> 00:06:19.720
of the original distribution relative to the new one.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.560
If you've optimized a compression scheme around one context,

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:25.880
how does it perform in another context?

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:30.434
Even just as I've stated things so far, perhaps this already rings a few bells

00:06:30.434 --> 00:06:33.920
in your mind as you think back to that zipping and language tree example.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:37.036
Before spelling that out, it is worth our time to take a few

00:06:37.036 --> 00:06:39.980
minutes generalizing this and packaging it into a couple formulas.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:44.532
Let's label all the probabilities from our new distribution with the letter P,

00:06:44.532 --> 00:06:48.180
and then all of those from the original distribution with the letter Q.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:51.691
And instead of limiting ourselves to four robot instructions,

00:06:51.691 --> 00:06:55.920
let's consider the general case of messages that look like any sequence of symbols.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:58.240
Those symbols could be the four robot instructions,

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.333
they could be the characters of English text,

00:07:00.333 --> 00:07:02.380
or they could be anything else you dream up.

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:06.378
An encoding optimized for this first distribution, Q,

00:07:06.378 --> 00:07:10.400
allocates negative log base 2 of Q sub i bits to each symbol.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:15.220
So the average bits used per instruction looks like this weighted sum.

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:19.127
Basically, you go through each symbol, and for each one you take the

00:07:19.127 --> 00:07:22.316
proportion of times that it comes up, which is its probability,

00:07:22.316 --> 00:07:26.500
multiplied by the information content, the negative log base 2, of its probability.

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.739
This sum was the other key term we had in the last lesson,

00:07:30.739 --> 00:07:34.700
the entropy of Q, and we visualized it as the area of this diagram.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:37.760
Each bar represents one part of the weighted sum.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:41.920
Its width is Q, and its height is the negative log base 2 of Q.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:47.375
But if the distribution that arises in practice is something else, P,

00:07:47.375 --> 00:07:50.137
and we keep using an encoding optimized for Q,

00:07:50.137 --> 00:07:55.190
then the average number of bits used per instruction would still look like a weighted

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:58.540
sum, but now the weights are these new probabilities, P.

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:03.598
So this general expression is, by definition, the cross

00:08:03.598 --> 00:08:07.880
entropy of the distribution Q relative to the distribution P.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:12.007
The specific notation that you'll see out in the wild is a little bit of a mess,

00:08:12.007 --> 00:08:13.660
there's a lot of different conventions.

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:19.349
For you and me, we'll just think about the sum itself, visualized with this diagram,

00:08:19.349 --> 00:08:24.996
where the width of each bar is P sub i, while the height is the negative log base 2 of Q

00:08:24.996 --> 00:08:25.496
sub i.

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:29.740
With any new formula, it helps to build some intuition with very simple examples.

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:34.506
So in this case, instead of having distributions with many possible events,

00:08:34.506 --> 00:08:37.000
consider a distribution with only two events.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:42.887
And let's say Q is an even distribution that assigns a 50% chance to each event,

00:08:42.887 --> 00:08:47.500
while P is more skewed, assigning 90% to one and 10% to the other.

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:52.398
This would mean, from Q's perspective, each event has one bit of information,

00:08:52.398 --> 00:08:56.679
and an optimal code for symbols following this distribution would spend

00:08:56.679 --> 00:09:00.960
only one bit on each such symbol, so the total entropy of Q is one bit.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:02.520
And what about cross entropy?

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:06.116
If this code was used when a new distribution of symbols applies,

00:09:06.116 --> 00:09:09.340
what would the average number of bits per symbol be in that case?

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:14.187
Well, in this example, actually, because each symbol has the same amount of information,

00:09:14.187 --> 00:09:18.427
one bit, the weights in the weighted sum don't actually make any difference,

00:09:18.427 --> 00:09:20.300
so cross entropy is also one bit.

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:22.220
But now think about flipping this around.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:26.333
Suppose that Q was the very skewed distribution with the 90-10 split,

00:09:26.333 --> 00:09:29.420
while P was an even distribution with the 50-50 split.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:34.735
Now, from Q's perspective, that one much more likely event has very little information,

00:09:34.735 --> 00:09:37.160
and the other one has a lot of information.

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:42.068
So as far as Q is concerned, a perfect code for symbols following this distribution

00:09:42.068 --> 00:09:46.325
should spend much less than one bit, again, averaged over all messages,

00:09:46.325 --> 00:09:51.233
for that one common symbol, trading off spending multiple bits on average for that

00:09:51.233 --> 00:09:52.120
second symbol.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:56.480
When you calculate it, the total entropy of Q is now smaller than one bit.

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:58.740
But now what about cross entropy?

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:02.834
If a code optimized for this skewed distribution was faced against

00:10:02.834 --> 00:10:05.777
a new reality where both events are equally likely,

00:10:05.777 --> 00:10:10.360
now you really do see a disadvantage, and that weighted sum is around 1.74 bits.

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:13.120
So stepping back, notice how much order matters here.

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:17.811
When we swapped the roles of P and Q, the value of this cross entropy changed,

00:10:17.811 --> 00:10:18.880
which makes sense.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:21.500
In the formula, the roles of P and Q are pretty different.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:25.235
While we're here, by the way, just showing simple distributions

00:10:25.235 --> 00:10:27.840
over only two possible outcomes, we can have some more fun.

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:32.896
Notice how this distribution Q depends on only one free variable, little q sub 1,

00:10:32.896 --> 00:10:37.160
and the complementary value has to be locked at 1 minus little q sub 1.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:38.900
And same deal for P.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:42.160
It really depends on just one parameter, little p sub 1.

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:45.761
The reason we can have some fun is that with so few free parameters,

00:10:45.761 --> 00:10:47.978
we can build more intuition by graphing things,

00:10:47.978 --> 00:10:50.380
which lets me highlight one critical property here.

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:54.164
I want you to imagine fixing P in place and then plotting

00:10:54.164 --> 00:10:57.080
this cross entropy expression as a function of Q.

00:10:57.560 --> 00:11:02.373
What you'll notice is this graph reaches a very clear minimum, and more specifically,

00:11:02.373 --> 00:11:06.960
the value where it achieves that minimum is the one where P and Q are identical.

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:11.420
And hopefully based on how we motivated it, this should make abundant sense.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:14.060
Always remember that question cross entropy is asking.

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:19.420
How well does a code optimized for one setting, Q, perform in a different setting, P?

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:24.160
That compression efficiency will obviously be at its best when both settings align.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:28.240
There's something very fun we can do with this plot that helps to highlight a key point.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:30.866
What I'm going to do is move around that value P,

00:11:30.866 --> 00:11:33.974
which changes the entire shape of the graph, and as I do,

00:11:33.974 --> 00:11:37.940
you'll notice I'm tracing out where that minimum value of the graph goes.

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:41.181
That traced out shape is a new curve that I'm drawing in green,

00:11:41.181 --> 00:11:44.380
and let me take a moment to ask you, what does this new curve represent?

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:49.142
Well, that minimum value for cross entropy is the most efficient

00:11:49.142 --> 00:11:52.457
possible compression when reality follows the distribution P,

00:11:52.457 --> 00:11:56.040
and that's exactly what the entropy of P is supposed to represent.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:59.575
And indeed, in this very simplified setting where we're dealing with

00:11:59.575 --> 00:12:02.070
a distribution over only two possible outcomes,

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:05.812
this green curve is what the entropy of P would look like as a function

00:12:05.812 --> 00:12:06.800
of little p sub 1.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:12.743
This graph emphasizes the one key point about cross entropy that I want

00:12:12.743 --> 00:12:16.937
you to remember from this video, which is that if you think of P as fixed

00:12:16.937 --> 00:12:21.753
and Q as the variable, it takes on its smallest possible value when Q is equal to P,

00:12:21.753 --> 00:12:25.380
and more specifically, that smallest value is the entropy of P.

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:28.667
It's a little harder to visualize in general, because usually these

00:12:28.667 --> 00:12:31.904
are distributions over many more than just two possible outcomes,

00:12:31.904 --> 00:12:35.240
but here's one way that you might hold that same fact in your head.

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:39.303
We'll take that core diagram that we built up, I'm going to keep P,

00:12:39.303 --> 00:12:43.420
the width of all the bars, fixed, and the distribution Q is going to be variable.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:46.809
Remember, Q is what determines the height of all of these bars,

00:12:46.809 --> 00:12:48.400
those negative log base 2 values.

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:53.118
And again, that key fact is that this value is bound by a certain minimum,

00:12:53.118 --> 00:12:56.166
that it achieves that minimum when Q is equal to P,

00:12:56.166 --> 00:12:58.980
and that the minimal value is the entropy of P.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:04.384
Before explaining why this key property makes cross entropy such a good fit for training

00:13:04.384 --> 00:13:08.746
large language models, I want you to take a moment to think about how that opening

00:13:08.746 --> 00:13:13.160
example of zipping and language trees is basically an application of cross entropy.

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:17.363
Remember what the key idea there was, where if you want to measure how

00:13:17.363 --> 00:13:20.816
different two different text documents are, we'll call them A and B,

00:13:20.816 --> 00:13:24.720
you take a little snippet of B, tack it onto A, and then compress the result.

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:27.901
You then compare the size of that compressed file to

00:13:27.901 --> 00:13:30.680
the size of what you get just compressing A on its own.

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:34.139
Again, because the zipping in that first context is mostly

00:13:34.139 --> 00:13:37.007
optimized for the patterns of document A, computing this

00:13:37.007 --> 00:13:40.480
difference is basically asking the same question cross entropy does.

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:43.472
How well does a compression scheme optimized for

00:13:43.472 --> 00:13:46.400
one context perform when faced with another context?

00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:49.746
I am glossing over a couple details where the specific distance metric

00:13:49.746 --> 00:13:52.901
that the authors defined was a little more complicated than this,

00:13:52.901 --> 00:13:56.200
but the core of it really is this difference that I've been showing.

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:59.760
Now this is not exactly cross entropy for a number of reasons.

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:03.100
One, it's not comparing two different probability distributions.

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:06.676
Instead, it's kind of like retreating a text document from one

00:14:06.676 --> 00:14:09.711
language as a sample from some distribution, and hence this

00:14:09.711 --> 00:14:13.100
difference is kind of like an empirical estimate of cross entropy.

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:16.670
And even then, it can't be a very good estimate because gzip

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:19.480
is not perfect compression achieving the Shannon limit.

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:20.580
Very far from it.

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:25.214
The algorithm underlying it is a much more straightforward way to find repetitions of

00:14:25.214 --> 00:14:29.720
a given text sequence and replace later instances with pointers to earlier instances.

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:34.189
But the author's idea was that, insofar as this approximates cross entropy,

00:14:34.189 --> 00:14:37.453
you can still get a useful distance measure, letting you do a

00:14:37.453 --> 00:14:40.560
legitimately interesting natural language processing task.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:44.520
This is a common theme for how cross entropy gets used in practice.

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:48.147
It comes up whenever you have a feeling of wanting to quantify how

00:14:48.147 --> 00:14:51.720
different the patterns in one setting are from those in another.

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:55.647
Here, that means the patterns of one language compared to that of another,

00:14:55.647 --> 00:14:58.740
but this transitions nicely to our primary application for today,

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:00.380
which is training language models.

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:05.130
Here, what you want to quantify is how different a model's understanding of language is

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:09.580
from the true patterns of language, at least as represented by a bunch of training data.

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:12.023
This animation I've been flashing, by the way,

00:15:12.023 --> 00:15:15.860
shows the underlying architecture of a transformer It's something a friend of mine made.

00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:19.953
But moving forward, I'm going to switch to something a little bit more schematic,

00:15:19.953 --> 00:15:23.920
simply representing a black box controlled by some large number of tunable parameters.

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:27.408
I'm assuming most viewers will have some familiarity

00:15:27.408 --> 00:15:29.320
with language models and neural networks.

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:31.720
These are topics that we have hit on on the channel before.

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:35.440
But here's a very quick recap of the basics for what you need to know.

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:38.425
Modern language models divide text into tokens,

00:15:38.425 --> 00:15:40.740
which are typically words or pieces of words.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:43.931
And you think of a language model as a function,

00:15:43.931 --> 00:15:47.509
a function that takes as an input any possible piece of text,

00:15:47.509 --> 00:15:52.069
thought of as a sequence of tokens, and it produces as an output a probability

00:15:52.069 --> 00:15:54.320
distribution over all possible tokens.

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:57.840
And you think of that output as its prediction for what might come next.

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:02.017
To train one of these models, what you need is a loss function,

00:16:02.017 --> 00:16:06.297
a way to quantify whether the predictions that it makes are generally good or

00:16:06.297 --> 00:16:07.120
generally bad.

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:11.250
A loose intuition, you might imagine, is the graph of a loss function as

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:14.960
something like this, some surface where you're hunting for the minimum.

00:16:15.380 --> 00:16:19.937
But you have to take any image like this with a heavy grain of salt because this is a

00:16:19.937 --> 00:16:23.154
graph of a function with two inputs, the variables x and y,

00:16:23.154 --> 00:16:27.443
whereas a true loss function takes as its input the many billions of parameters

00:16:27.443 --> 00:16:29.320
characterizing the language model.

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:33.480
So the real image we want to look at here would have to live in billions of dimensions.

00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:36.680
Still, it's enough to reference the core idea,

00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:40.490
which is how training looks like tweaking and tuning these models in

00:16:40.490 --> 00:16:45.460
a somewhat automatic way to take steps down this hill, decreasing the value of that loss.

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:48.452
The automatic process for this tweaking and tuning

00:16:48.452 --> 00:16:50.940
comes down to gradient descent and backpropagation.

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:53.480
I have done videos on these if you want the details.

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:56.969
But the point I want to emphasize is that for you, the engineer,

00:16:56.969 --> 00:17:01.300
trying to train a model, once you define a good loss function, you're essentially done.

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:04.099
The standard machinery of deep learning takes it from there.

00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:08.211
The loss you define is with respect to some big pile of training data,

00:17:08.211 --> 00:17:11.318
which in the language model context would look like many,

00:17:11.318 --> 00:17:14.319
many samples of text typically drawn from the Internet.

00:17:14.920 --> 00:17:19.026
So the key design question for you is what mathematical function can you write

00:17:19.026 --> 00:17:22.447
down describing how well the model performs on all of this data,

00:17:22.447 --> 00:17:26.500
such that the act of minimizing that function is the same thing as improving

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:27.500
model performance.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:32.300
A very nice way to think about the loss function we use in this case is that

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:36.160
you measure the average information per token from the model's perspective.

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:37.980
Here's what I mean by that.

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:40.630
For a given example snippet of text in that data,

00:17:40.630 --> 00:17:45.120
you would compute the probability that the model gives to each new token in there.

00:17:45.640 --> 00:17:48.987
That is, for every initial subsequence of tokens, like this one,

00:17:48.987 --> 00:17:51.549
you have the model predict what would come next,

00:17:51.549 --> 00:17:55.419
and then you look at the true next token, the one that actually showed up

00:17:55.419 --> 00:17:59.288
in this training example, and you consider what probability did the model

00:17:59.288 --> 00:18:01.380
give to that one, that true next token.

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:04.150
That probability is what I'm representing with the

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:06.380
little pie charts below each one of these tokens.

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:09.415
The models are actually specifically designed to give

00:18:09.415 --> 00:18:12.220
you all of these probabilities on a single pass.

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:16.868
When I say the loss is the average information from the model's perspective,

00:18:16.868 --> 00:18:20.400
what I mean is we take the negative log of all these probabilities.

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:23.528
As we've discussed multiple times by now, you can

00:18:23.528 --> 00:18:26.320
think of that as measuring how surprising an event is.

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:30.535
You would expect a smarter model that's really following what's going on to

00:18:30.535 --> 00:18:34.249
generally be unsurprised by what it sees, meaning it gives the true next

00:18:34.249 --> 00:18:38.217
token's higher probabilities, so all these information values would be lower,

00:18:38.217 --> 00:18:41.880
while a randomized or poorly trained model would just be very confused.

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:45.260
It would give everything a low probability and be surprised all the time.

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:49.403
Pulling up that graph of the negative log that we were playing with last time,

00:18:49.403 --> 00:18:52.440
this is hopefully very intuitive as a choice for the loss function.

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:56.675
It's effectively telling you the model is punished, and punished very steeply,

00:18:56.675 --> 00:18:59.658
if it assigns a very low probability to the true next token,

00:18:59.658 --> 00:19:03.668
whereas if that probability is high, that prediction would contribute only a tiny

00:19:03.668 --> 00:19:04.940
amount to the total loss.

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:09.531
But the thing is, a lot of functions have this same general qualitative shape,

00:19:09.531 --> 00:19:13.076
so a question that I want to be hanging in your mind right now is

00:19:13.076 --> 00:19:16.300
why information specifically would be the best choice here.

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:20.000
What is it that makes logs special among all the functions with this shape?

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:23.944
One small nuance is that in machine learning, you almost

00:19:23.944 --> 00:19:26.400
always use the natural log instead of a log base 2.

00:19:26.840 --> 00:19:30.152
This doesn't really matter, the two expressions differ by a constant factor,

00:19:30.152 --> 00:19:33.420
so it's all getting absorbed in something called the learning rate anyway.

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:36.872
The motive is just that natural logs are a lot cleaner whenever you're

00:19:36.872 --> 00:19:40.324
taking derivatives, and derivatives are the core part of the gradient

00:19:40.324 --> 00:19:44.220
descent algorithm used to concretely decrease the value of this loss function.

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:47.400
And in some sense, that's it.

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:49.180
That's really all there is to pre-training.

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:51.799
All you're doing is looking through each token,

00:19:51.799 --> 00:19:54.998
taking the negative log of the probability the model gives to it,

00:19:54.998 --> 00:19:57.180
and averaging that over every token you see.

00:19:57.580 --> 00:20:00.127
And of course, it's not every token in just one example,

00:20:00.127 --> 00:20:03.083
it's every token in the entirety of the vast, vast training set,

00:20:03.083 --> 00:20:07.040
which you hope represents a significant sample of the language you're trying to model.

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:09.940
Of course, there really is more to it.

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:13.129
I've glossed over things like batching or specific optimizers,

00:20:13.129 --> 00:20:17.263
and getting this to run at the mind-boggling scales used in practice is a complete

00:20:17.263 --> 00:20:18.160
engineering feat.

00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:21.457
But at the level of abstraction of just thinking of loss functions,

00:20:21.457 --> 00:20:22.860
it really is remarkably simple.

00:20:23.020 --> 00:20:25.628
It really is just the average information per token,

00:20:25.628 --> 00:20:29.740
assuming all the machinery of deep learning can do its job to minimize this loss,

00:20:29.740 --> 00:20:33.401
hopefully getting it to approach something like the entropy of language,

00:20:33.401 --> 00:20:36.560
what you'd be left with would be a powerful general predictor.

00:20:38.020 --> 00:20:42.753
You might think this loss function would go by a name like information loss, or log loss,

00:20:42.753 --> 00:20:45.306
but in fact, people call it cross-entropy loss,

00:20:45.306 --> 00:20:50.039
which at first might sound really strange, given that nowhere in the explanation have we

00:20:50.039 --> 00:20:52.220
actually used the cross-entropy formula.

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:57.019
Now there is one very unsatisfying way that this is commonly described,

00:20:57.019 --> 00:20:59.820
where you say, we are calculating cross-entropy,

00:20:59.820 --> 00:21:02.906
but it's between the model's output on a given token,

00:21:02.906 --> 00:21:07.194
and a fully skewed distribution with all the probability mass concentrated

00:21:07.194 --> 00:21:09.080
on just the one true next token.

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:13.107
If you pull up the formula we had earlier, where we're taking a weighted sum,

00:21:13.107 --> 00:21:15.435
using all these quantities on the right as weights,

00:21:15.435 --> 00:21:17.584
all of the zeros cancel most of the things out,

00:21:17.584 --> 00:21:20.628
and all you're left with is the negative log of the probability the

00:21:20.628 --> 00:21:22.240
model gives to the true next token.

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:26.080
But if that's all there was to it, this would be very lame, at least in my opinion.

00:21:26.380 --> 00:21:30.121
This does nothing to explain why you're using cross-entropy in the first place,

00:21:30.121 --> 00:21:33.720
and if it all just evaporates away anyway, why not just be straightforward?

00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:36.860
Why not just call it log loss, or information loss?

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:40.912
Let me show you what I find more satisfying, which is to start by

00:21:40.912 --> 00:21:44.480
explaining why the choice of logarithms here is not really a choice at all.

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:47.641
Your hand is almost forced into it, and when you see how,

00:21:47.641 --> 00:21:51.480
the formula for cross-entropy just naturally pops right out in front of you.

00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:54.960
So far we've only focused on one example at a time,

00:21:54.960 --> 00:21:57.815
one specific token prediction from one snippet of text,

00:21:57.815 --> 00:22:01.893
and in our framing we want to turn the probability that the model gives to that

00:22:01.893 --> 00:22:05.869
token into some kind of loss, some value, telling the model how bad it should

00:22:05.869 --> 00:22:08.520
feel and how aggressively it should correct itself.

00:22:09.060 --> 00:22:11.342
Now at first, it looks like the only real constraint

00:22:11.342 --> 00:22:13.360
is that this should be a decreasing function.

00:22:13.700 --> 00:22:16.937
You want to assign high loss to low probabilities,

00:22:16.937 --> 00:22:19.720
and low loss to probabilities closer to 1.

00:22:20.140 --> 00:22:23.032
So given the infinite abundance of functions with this rough shape,

00:22:23.032 --> 00:22:25.060
what makes one of them better than the others?

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:28.107
Let's say we don't know right now, so whatever choice we make,

00:22:28.107 --> 00:22:30.797
we're going to label it as F, and see if we can find some other

00:22:30.797 --> 00:22:33.740
property that we want F to have, something that might force our hand.

00:22:34.600 --> 00:22:37.700
And for this, imagine an example like myName is blank.

00:22:38.200 --> 00:22:41.270
This is a small enough and common enough pattern that you would be likely

00:22:41.270 --> 00:22:44.340
to see many different instances of it show up in your pre-training data.

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:48.560
What you might imagine is very common names show up multiple times in the data,

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:50.580
while rare names show up less frequently.

00:22:50.580 --> 00:22:53.711
When you feed in these tokens, myName is, into the model,

00:22:53.711 --> 00:22:57.062
it produces some distribution over all possible next tokens,

00:22:57.062 --> 00:23:00.797
and so let's label the probabilities in the model's distribution of

00:23:00.797 --> 00:23:02.280
outputs with the letter Q.

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:06.940
Now let me ask you, what is the total loss associated with this prediction?

00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:10.837
If you were just focused on one instance where this pattern shows up in

00:23:10.837 --> 00:23:14.303
the training data, that loss looks like F of Q for whatever your choice

00:23:14.303 --> 00:23:17.720
of the function F is, whether that's a negative log or something else.

00:23:17.720 --> 00:23:22.140
But the key is that this is only one part of the total loss associated with the example.

00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:26.035
Every other instance of this name also contributes that same amount,

00:23:26.035 --> 00:23:29.942
and each instance of every other name also contributes an analogous amount,

00:23:29.942 --> 00:23:34.260
except where you input the probabilities that the model gives to those other names.

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:37.375
Here, I'll use the letter P to represent the proportion

00:23:37.375 --> 00:23:40.280
of times that a given name shows up in the training data.

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:44.849
The average value for the loss of the model's output over all of the examples

00:23:44.849 --> 00:23:49.140
that have this pattern looks like a weighted average, weighted by these values P.

00:23:49.860 --> 00:23:52.680
From here, maybe you can start to anticipate the role of cross entropy.

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:56.675
If you choose to make this function F the negative log of Q,

00:23:56.675 --> 00:24:00.233
then this full expression for the total average loss associated

00:24:00.233 --> 00:24:04.180
with the input text really is exactly the definition of cross entropy.

00:24:04.780 --> 00:24:08.382
Specifically, it's the cross entropy of our model's output

00:24:08.382 --> 00:24:11.860
relative to the statistics represented within the data.

00:24:12.220 --> 00:24:15.580
And remember the one key property of this formula I want you to remember.

00:24:15.580 --> 00:24:18.388
It's something that takes on a minimum exactly when

00:24:18.388 --> 00:24:21.140
all these P and Q values are equal to each other.

00:24:21.620 --> 00:24:25.714
So in this setting, that would mean it's minimized exactly when the distribution your

00:24:25.714 --> 00:24:29.760
model outputs is the same as the statistical distribution represented in your data.

00:24:30.300 --> 00:24:33.720
But the real key point is that this implication goes the other way around.

00:24:33.860 --> 00:24:38.289
If we switch back to writing this per example loss with some generic function F,

00:24:38.289 --> 00:24:43.162
and you say you would like it to be the case that the full average loss over all of the

00:24:43.162 --> 00:24:47.979
examples is minimized only when the model's output matches the statistics that show up

00:24:47.979 --> 00:24:52.851
in the data, there's actually a really nice mathematical argument for why F has to be a

00:24:52.851 --> 00:24:53.460
logarithm.

00:24:54.040 --> 00:24:56.898
I'm hesitant to derail us too much by delving into the details,

00:24:56.898 --> 00:24:59.394
which involves something called a Lagrange multiplier,

00:24:59.394 --> 00:25:01.980
since there's a risk of losing the forest for the trees.

00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:05.759
I know many of you are curious, so I will pull up the details on the screen

00:25:05.759 --> 00:25:09.320
for any multivariable calculus enthusiasts interested in pausing and pondering.

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:11.780
The high-level idea really is very pretty.

00:25:11.780 --> 00:25:15.605
It's that to minimize our key expression, subject to the condition that the

00:25:15.605 --> 00:25:19.584
sum of all the q values is equal to one, you end up with a problem that looks

00:25:19.584 --> 00:25:23.460
like finding values where two different contours are tangent to each other.

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:28.443
You compute where that happens by setting a pair of gradient values equal to each other,

00:25:28.443 --> 00:25:32.275
and this, for our specific expressions, will boil down to the condition

00:25:32.275 --> 00:25:36.160
that the derivative of our function F with respect to q has to look like

00:25:36.160 --> 00:25:39.460
some constant over q, and only logarithms have that property.

00:25:39.460 --> 00:25:42.020
Again, I pull this up just for the extra curious.

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:44.080
The details are not too important.

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:47.335
The main thing I want you to know is that if you want a loss

00:25:47.335 --> 00:25:50.299
function with the property that it's minimized only when the

00:25:50.299 --> 00:25:53.360
model matches the statistics of the data, your hand is forced.

00:25:53.560 --> 00:25:55.440
You actually have to use the negative log.

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:58.910
And in this case, the average loss associated with any given

00:25:58.910 --> 00:26:01.960
input text looks exactly like the cross-entropy formula.

00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:05.426
And while I'm here throwing up notes for the extra curious,

00:26:05.426 --> 00:26:08.309
here's another one about overfitting and how magical it is that this

00:26:08.309 --> 00:26:11.820
actually works when you stop and think about the sparsity of the data that we have.

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:16.656
The last topic I want to hit on in this section is an interesting variant on

00:26:16.656 --> 00:26:20.300
training language models where this cross-entropy formula is used more explicitly.

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:25.428
And it's a case where each training example gives a much softer tug on the model's

00:26:25.428 --> 00:26:29.440
distributions, one that is not so over-weighted to one specific next token.

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:31.160
It's something called distillation.

00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:36.646
The setup here is that you might want to train a relatively small model where its

00:26:36.646 --> 00:26:41.240
goal is to approximate the performance of some larger, presumably much smarter model.

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:45.974
This comes up all the time, because very often after getting some desired performance by

00:26:45.974 --> 00:26:50.094
scaling up all of your parameters, what you would love is to be able to approximate that

00:26:50.094 --> 00:26:54.260
performance with something much smaller and much more efficient to run at inference time.

00:26:54.940 --> 00:26:58.911
The idea is that with a bigger model in hand, you have the ability to do something much,

00:26:58.911 --> 00:27:01.980
much more efficient than normal pre-training with that small model.

00:27:02.340 --> 00:27:06.122
What you do is look through a bunch of data, and as before, at each new token,

00:27:06.122 --> 00:27:10.340
you consider the small model's prediction of what will follow, that full distribution.

00:27:11.040 --> 00:27:14.816
But now, instead of just comparing it to the true next token in the data,

00:27:14.816 --> 00:27:17.350
you compare it to the larger model's prediction,

00:27:17.350 --> 00:27:20.660
its full distribution across all the tokens at that same point.

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:24.640
You define the loss at this token to be the cross-entropy of

00:27:24.640 --> 00:27:28.160
the small model's distribution relative to the big model's.

00:27:28.740 --> 00:27:31.393
When you think about it, this gives you something much,

00:27:31.393 --> 00:27:34.625
much richer than ordinary pre-training, since pre-training is just

00:27:34.625 --> 00:27:38.580
giving full weight to only one true next token, the one that happens to be there.

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:42.634
As an analogy, it's the difference between trying to learn chess just by

00:27:42.634 --> 00:27:46.377
watching a game versus having someone better than you talk through all the

00:27:46.377 --> 00:27:50.220
possible good moves and how heavily they would weight them at each new turn.

00:27:51.840 --> 00:27:54.205
Looking back at that My Name is Blank example,

00:27:54.205 --> 00:27:58.370
instead of having to see many thousands or many millions of examples in the data

00:27:58.370 --> 00:28:02.638
before your loss represents the cross-entropy against a reasonable distribution of

00:28:02.638 --> 00:28:06.289
possible names, you can get that same effect just on a single example,

00:28:06.289 --> 00:28:08.860
comparing it against the good model's prediction.

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:14.440
I think now is a good time to zoom out and look over everything we've built up.

00:28:14.840 --> 00:28:17.362
In the first half, in the context of compression,

00:28:17.362 --> 00:28:21.481
cross-entropy arose very naturally just by asking how well a compression scheme

00:28:21.481 --> 00:28:23.900
optimized for one setting performs in another.

00:28:24.760 --> 00:28:28.739
The mild surprise is that this idea turns out to be much more generally useful,

00:28:28.739 --> 00:28:33.020
as a way to measure how different patterns in one setting are from those in another.

00:28:33.420 --> 00:28:36.900
This is what makes it such a common tool for loss functions in modern machine learning.

00:28:37.520 --> 00:28:41.445
All that said, as stated so far, the way that the formula showed up in the second half,

00:28:41.445 --> 00:28:45.100
for us, feels pretty different from the way that it showed up in the first half.

00:28:45.400 --> 00:28:47.360
It was essentially unrelated to compression.

00:28:47.660 --> 00:28:50.546
The logic actually came from a completely different part of math,

00:28:50.546 --> 00:28:51.700
constrained optimization.

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:54.360
But I wouldn't be putting these two topics in the same

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:56.680
video if they didn't have a tighter connection than that.

00:28:56.680 --> 00:28:59.411
In the same way that whenever you see pi show up in math,

00:28:59.411 --> 00:29:03.579
even when it seems unrelated to circles, there's always going to be some connection to

00:29:03.579 --> 00:29:06.885
circles hiding there, I think whenever you see cross-entropy pop up,

00:29:06.885 --> 00:29:09.280
there will be a way to connect it to compression.

00:29:10.300 --> 00:29:13.664
And in the case of language models, we've already teed things up for how

00:29:13.664 --> 00:29:16.982
to make that connection a little bit more tight by describing the loss

00:29:16.982 --> 00:29:20.580
function as the average information per token, from the model's perspective.

00:29:21.640 --> 00:29:24.660
The next step from here is to explain how it's possible

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:27.240
to turn a general predictor into a compressor.

00:29:27.660 --> 00:29:30.874
For instance, turning a language model, this prediction machine,

00:29:30.874 --> 00:29:32.280
into a language compressor.

00:29:32.640 --> 00:29:35.778
More specifically, one where the number of bits that you use

00:29:35.778 --> 00:29:38.812
to represent a piece of text will approximately match the

00:29:38.812 --> 00:29:42.160
information content of that text, from the model's perspective.

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:44.620
It's actually very surprising this is even possible.

00:29:44.980 --> 00:29:48.395
There's one very visual and very beautiful compression algorithm showing how

00:29:48.395 --> 00:29:51.720
to do exactly this that you and I are going to dig into in the next part.

00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:56.137
And once you understand this, it makes clear how using cross-entropy loss is

00:29:56.137 --> 00:30:00.720
actually equivalent to training the model to be the best possible text compressor.

00:30:01.320 --> 00:30:05.776
This, in turn, gives us a nice hill to stand on where we can look over the landscape

00:30:05.776 --> 00:30:10.020
and assess that thought-provoking phrase, compression is intelligence, head-on.

00:30:12.100 --> 00:30:14.558
I do want to add one final footnote to this video,

00:30:14.558 --> 00:30:18.393
because it would be a shame not to at least quickly explain a related concept

00:30:18.393 --> 00:30:22.031
called KL divergence, which you also see all the time littered throughout

00:30:22.031 --> 00:30:23.260
machine learning papers.

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:27.148
Before I jump into that, I want to make a very quick plug for 3b1b talent,

00:30:27.148 --> 00:30:31.560
because there's actually a puzzle on that page that is directly related to KL divergence.

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:36.189
For context, this is something like a virtual career fair that I've put together this

00:30:36.189 --> 00:30:40.409
year, where the premise is to have a set of partnered organizations that are interesting

00:30:40.409 --> 00:30:43.822
places to work, who are also keen to hire the kinds of people like you,

00:30:43.822 --> 00:30:47.756
people who watch videos about things like the relationship between compression and

00:30:47.756 --> 00:30:48.420
intelligence.

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:52.139
One of the main features of that page is a set of interviews that I did with

00:30:52.139 --> 00:30:55.521
the various teams, which I think is just a much better way to let you get to

00:30:55.521 --> 00:30:58.860
know the culture of a company than trying to just read a generic jobs page.

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:02.300
But another thing you'll find is that many companies there have

00:31:02.300 --> 00:31:04.892
featured challenges specifically for this audience,

00:31:04.892 --> 00:31:08.480
where you are often invited to mention your solution in an application.

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:11.530
Now, this was not at all planned, but by happenstance,

00:31:11.530 --> 00:31:14.622
the challenge that one of the companies, Dopple, put up there,

00:31:14.622 --> 00:31:16.880
happens to use the formula for KL divergence.

00:31:17.380 --> 00:31:20.927
They didn't know I was planning this video, so you can take this as a nice bit of

00:31:20.927 --> 00:31:24.780
evidence that the math I'm teaching you here really does show up out in the real world.

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:29.660
This is just one example, but you can find a number of other interesting places

00:31:29.660 --> 00:31:34.180
to work with nice puzzles and challenges for you to try out at 3b1b.co/talent.

00:31:35.300 --> 00:31:37.080
Okay, so what is KL divergence?

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:41.440
Remember that key property of cross entropy that I want you to come away remembering?

00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:45.673
The fact that it takes on a minimal value when p and q are the same,

00:31:45.673 --> 00:31:48.680
and that that minimum is equal to the entropy of p.

00:31:49.140 --> 00:31:51.750
This makes it very natural to want to give a name to

00:31:51.750 --> 00:31:54.260
describe the difference between these two values.

00:31:54.740 --> 00:31:57.353
And indeed, this difference has the special name,

00:31:57.353 --> 00:32:01.460
it is the Kolbach-Leibler divergence, commonly abbreviated to KL divergence.

00:32:02.040 --> 00:32:05.710
In the language of compression, you would think of it as describing how

00:32:05.710 --> 00:32:09.380
many bits per symbol are you wasting by using a poorly optimized code.

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:13.683
For example, in our robot case, if you take the bits per symbol in the

00:32:13.683 --> 00:32:17.136
unoptimized case minus the bits per symbol in the optimized case,

00:32:17.136 --> 00:32:21.060
that difference gives you the KL divergence between the two distributions.

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:24.260
It's specifically telling you how wasteful you are.

00:32:24.860 --> 00:32:27.194
In the machine learning setting, KL divergence acts

00:32:27.194 --> 00:32:29.620
as a kind of distance measure between distributions.

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:33.580
It's zero when both of them are the same, but it grows as they differ.

00:32:33.940 --> 00:32:36.940
It's not exactly a difference though, because it's asymmetric.

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:42.260
The KL divergence of q relative to p is not the same as that of p relative to q.

00:32:42.620 --> 00:32:44.767
To cap off today, I want to leave you with three

00:32:44.767 --> 00:32:46.780
things to ponder related to this expression.

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:49.830
The first one is easy, which is how when you see it in practice,

00:32:49.830 --> 00:32:52.542
it often comes up in this more compact form, and I just want you

00:32:52.542 --> 00:32:55.420
to convince yourself that these two expressions really are the same.

00:32:55.760 --> 00:32:58.641
The compact form hides the fact that it's really just a difference

00:32:58.641 --> 00:33:01.260
between cross entropy and entropy, but it is more succinct.

00:33:01.880 --> 00:33:05.540
Secondly, I've been showing this diagram, a variant on our cross entropy diagram.

00:33:05.820 --> 00:33:08.384
I want to see if you can take a moment to convince yourself

00:33:08.384 --> 00:33:10.600
in what sense this is representing our expression.

00:33:11.240 --> 00:33:15.027
And third, I mentioned that when you distill a large model into a smaller one,

00:33:15.027 --> 00:33:18.620
you use the cross entropy between their distributions as a loss function.

00:33:19.040 --> 00:33:22.660
I want you to ask yourself, what would happen if instead you used the KL divergence?

00:33:23.200 --> 00:33:25.941
After all, if the KL divergence is like a distance measure,

00:33:25.941 --> 00:33:27.800
wouldn't this be a more natural choice?

